window.docContents[80].resolve({"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization--Fixed-Point-Methods-for-Semilinear-Problems--Compactness-and-Monotonicity-Beyond-Contractions":{"contents":"Schauder's fixed-point theorem\n replaces metric contraction by continuity,\ncompactness, and invariance of a convex set. It can treat maps with no\nsmall-Lipschitz estimate, though it does not generally give uniqueness or a\nconvergent Picard iteration. Minty and Browder developed another direction:\nmonotonicity and coercivity of a nonlinear operator can replace compactness in\nexistence arguments \n. Schauder's theorem and Schaefer's theorem\nare proved for compact maps on real normed spaces. The validation argument below\nuses Schauder in a weaker topology to obtain existence near an approximate solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Fixed Points and Convex Regularization\u0009Fixed-Point Methods for Semilinear Problems","header":"9.6.1.2. Compactness and Monotonicity Beyond Contractions","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Fixed-Points-and-Convex-Regularization--Fixed-Point-Methods-for-Semilinear-Problems--Compactness-and-Monotonicity-Beyond-Contractions"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Composition___-truncation___-and-level-sets":{"contents":"For every , a zero-preserving real Lipschitz map acts on\nthe global first-order Sobolev space with the expected weak chain rule.  The\nproof treats the nonsmooth outer map through Rademacher differentiability,\nthe vanishing of weak gradients on level sets, and the ACL characterization.\n\nThe following are completed operations:\n\n* absolute value;* positive and negative parts;* interval truncation containing zero on the whole space;* arbitrary ordered interval truncation on bounded operational domains.\n\nTheir base coordinates and weak derivatives are identified almost everywhere.\nIn particular,\n\n\n\nalmost everywhere. The\nlocal chain rule\nholds on every open domain for all , with no boundary\nregularity assumption. A cutoff around each test function reduces it to\nthe whole-space calculation.\n\nTo obtain a global Sobolev element, the composed base function must also\nbelong to . A zero-preserving Lipschitz map always meets that condition.\nOn a finite-measure domain, every real Lipschitz map does, since its constant\noffset is integrable.\n\nLocal Sobolev membership needs no such global integrability condition.\nEvery real Lipschitz map sends  into itself,\nincluding  and . A compact neighborhood of each test\nfunction supplies the finite measure needed for a nonzero constant offset.\nThe resulting weak chain rule holds on arbitrary open domains.\n\nThe nonsmooth composition theory is scalar-valued.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.10. Composition, truncation, and level sets","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Composition___-truncation___-and-level-sets"}});